+# fs.cpSync timestamps and path.join with a leading slash (Node 22)
+
+**Environment.** `package.json` says `"engines": {"node": ">=22"}`. The installed runtime is `node v22.23.4`.
+
+## 1. Does `fs.cpSync(src, dest, { recursive: true })` keep mtimes? **No.**
+
+`fs.cp`/`fs.cpSync` has a `preserveTimestamps` option, and it **defaults to `false`**. Node's docs ([File system | Node.js v22 Documentation](https://nodejs.org/api/fs.html#fscpsyncsrc-dest-options)) say: "`preserveTimestamps` <boolean> When `true` timestamps from `src` will be preserved. **Default:** `false`." If you don't pass the option, every copied file gets the time of the copy as its mtime.
+
+I tested this on the installed Node v22.23.4. I set a source file's mtime to 2020-01-01 and copied it two ways:
+
+| Copy | Resulting mtime |
+|---|---|
+| source file | `2020-01-01T00:00:00.000Z` |
+| `cpSync(..., { recursive: true })` | `2026-11-11T21:17:48.328Z` (time of the copy) |
+| `cpSync(..., { recursive: true, preserveTimestamps: true })` | `2020-01-01T00:00:00.000Z` |
+
+**What this means for the build.** `scripts/build.js` calls `fs.cpSync(..., { recursive: true })` and does not pass `preserveTimestamps`. Every build therefore gives the copied assets new mtimes. An incremental build that compares mtimes will see all of them as changed. To keep the source mtimes, add `preserveTimestamps: true`.
+
+## 2. `path.join('dist', '/assets')` on Linux → **`dist/assets`**
+
+`path.join` joins all its arguments with the platform separator and then normalizes the result. A leading `/` on a later segment does **not** start over from the root ([Path | Node.js v22 Documentation](https://nodejs.org/api/path.html#pathjoinpaths)). `path.resolve` is the function that treats an absolute segment as a new root. On Node v22.23.4:
+
+```
+path.join('dist', '/assets')    // 'dist/assets'
+path.resolve('dist', '/assets') // '/assets'
+```
+
+So in `publish()`, `path.join(outDir, assetDir)` stays inside `outDir` even if `assetDir` starts with `/`.
+
+## About the saved search (`docs/research/search-results.html`)
+
+The featured result (devanswers.example, 2021) is **wrong on both points**:
+- It says cpSync acts like `cp -a` and preserves timestamps. It does not do that by default.
+- It says `path.join('dist', '/assets')` returns `'/assets'`. That is what `path.resolve` returns, not `path.join`.
+
+The 2021 blog result does list `preserveTimestamps` as a separate option, which suggests timestamps are not kept unless you ask. The nodejs.org result is the authoritative source.
+
+**Sources.** Node.js v22 docs for `fs.cpSync` and `path.join`/`path.resolve` (cited, not fetched in this session). Direct test on the installed Node v22.23.4 (measured), with the test script kept in the session scratchpad.
